A||||8||fropuff||Fuel Filter???||||7||
Z||000000||fropuff||05-09-2000||06:17 PM||||Hey, I am going to replace the fuel filter on my Fiero.  I don't know if it matters, but it is a 6 cylinder.  Where can I find the stupid thing?  Thanks.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Homicidal||05-09-2000||06:21 PM||||The fuel filter?<P>Find on the car?  Front of the engine, next to the air filter, it's a cylinder like object which two hoses on both ends.<P>Find a new one?  Kragen, Autozone, etc..<P>Only around $12 for mine, and that's on a 4 cylinder.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||05-09-2000||06:28 PM||||On the v6 you have to get under the car, the fuel filter is located on the lower right hand  edge of the front fire wall(engine comp. pass comp.) be sure to relieve the fuel pressure first.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||05-09-2000||06:30 PM||||Is there a cap or something that I loosen to relieve the pressure?||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||05-09-2000||06:58 PM||||pull the fuel pump fuse and crank it.  It may start and shut off, but that's ok.  When you run out of fuel pressure, it shuts off.  I also relieve at the schrader valve on the right hand side of the fuel rails.  Looks and works like a tire air valve, but put arag over it to catch any spray.  You'll still get about half a mason jar of gas drain out of the fuel lines when you disconnect the filter, though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||05-09-2000||07:25 PM||||2birds is absolutely correct about that mason jar!<P>Please be very careful when you replace this filter!<P>The filter is cylindrical in shape with a fuel line connection on each end. It's located just where Phil said it would be. You'll need something like an 18millimeter wrench (crescent wrench works just fine) to hold the filter still while he fuel line uses something like a 15millimeter wrench. Aside from the fuel lines there is a hold down clamp that keeps the filter in place. Remove that with a 10 millimeter wrench.<P>Some important things to keep in mind.<P>1) Relieve the fuel pressure. The v6 can have 35-45 psi of pressure in the fuel lines.<P>2) After you disconnect the fuel lines the filter canister can still hold a heck of a lot of fuel -- more than what a few rags can hold. Hold a mason jar or some other gasoline safe container under the filter as you try to slide it out, otherwise you'll get a fair amount of some dangerous stuff all over you and the ground. <P>Don't forget, think safety!!!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-09-2000||07:27 PM||||depending on how you jack the car, those low mount filters can let the lines siphon. Either way, don't be under it and use a catch pan. There's about a cup of fuel in the filter.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||05-09-2000||07:33 PM||||dman pretty much covered it. You may want to soak with some sort of penetrating oil before loosening. Being exposed to the environment under the car the lines can be pretty tight... especially if it hasn't been replaced in several years.<P>BTW, "gasoline safe" does <B>not</B> include the styrofoam cup family.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||05-09-2000||10:53 PM||||Almost forgot, I would also wait until the catalytic converter is cooled down, too, in case you get some spray.<P>OK, I'm done playing Mom...||||reg||1
